WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our next guest is
a talented comedian

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:05.972 line:0%
who has written a new book
called "The History of Stand-Up:

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:07.907 line:0%
From Mark Twain
to Dave Chappelle,"

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.008
which is available now.

00:00:09.008 --> 00:00:11.710
Please welcome Wayne Federman!

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:13.312
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:14.780
[ Chuckling ] Wow! Wow!

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.482
-Yes.
-This is --

00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:17.684
This is unbelievable.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.886
Thank you so much for coming on.

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:21.320
Wayne, it's great to see you.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.989
How have you -- How have you --
How have you been?

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.724
How is everything going?

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.793
-Well, it's actually
going pretty well.

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:28.694
I -- You know, everyone does

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:31.164
their own self-care
during the pandemic.

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:33.032
I saw Anna --
she did her exercising.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.770
And, of course,
I am doing, you know --

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:40.106
I microdose --
Claritin is my system.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:43.175
[ Laughter ]
-Wow, microdosing Claritin.

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:45.878
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
That's -- That's what I do.

00:00:45.878 --> 00:00:47.513
But it's been really fun.
It's been really fun.

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.615
Something crazy happened
during the pandemic to me.

00:00:49.615 --> 00:00:52.652
I had my identity stolen.
-No! Wayne, that's t--

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:54.153
-Yeah, yeah.
-That's terrible.

00:00:54.153 --> 00:00:57.390
-Yeah, so -- No, no, it actually
was one of the best things

00:00:57.390 --> 00:00:58.891
that happened to me,
'cause I go through life,

00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:00.860
and I wonder, you know,
"Do I matter?

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.728
Does any of this count?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:04.296
"Do I make a difference?"
Then one day I wake up,

00:01:04.296 --> 00:01:07.600
and someone's like,
"Hey, I want to be you, Wayne."

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.936
[ Laughter ]
-Wow.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.204
-Somebody wants to be me.
[ Scattered cheers ]

00:01:11.204 --> 00:01:12.838
Yeah.
-Wow, that's -- that's amazing.

00:01:12.838 --> 00:01:14.774
Now, I know
you and Anna Kendrick

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.475
worked on a project together.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.043
-Yes, we did.
-And I was gonna bring it up.

00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:19.145
-Yes, we did.
-What --

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:20.713
-It was on Quibi.
It was called "Dummy."

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:23.249
She played a woman
that could talk

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:25.284
to her boyfriend's sex doll.

00:01:25.284 --> 00:01:26.686
It was incredible.

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:28.855
And I, of course --
I think you can tell --

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:31.424
I played the sex-doll repairman
in that.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.626
[ Laughter ]
And what happens is

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.294
the show didn't get canceled --

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:38.330
the whole platform got canceled,
the whole network.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.199
[ Laughter ]
-Wow, that's a first.

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:41.967
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.
But, apparently,

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:44.504
Anna's been able to bounce back
pretty nicely, so, yes.

00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:47.573
[ Laughter ]
-I was looking

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.842
at your -- your credits online.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:52.845
-And, you know, you have
over 100 credits in movies.

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:55.614
What do you get recognized
the most for?

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:58.317
-Well, probably, maybe
"Curb Your Enthusiasm,"

00:01:58.317 --> 00:02:01.087
but, also, there's this thing
where I am in,

00:02:01.087 --> 00:02:03.456
like, a lot of movies,
but just in one scene.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:06.692
Like, in "Legally Blonde,"
I'm part of the Harvard Board

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:08.994
that lets Elle Woods
into college.

00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:10.295
[ Cheers and applause ]
Pivotal part of that movie,

00:02:10.295 --> 00:02:12.531
very important.
-I like that.

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:16.035
-I'm also the blind guy
in "Step Brothers."

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:18.971
I'm in one scene in a movie,
and then gone.

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:20.072
You never see me again.

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:21.774
I call it the
"Federman-and-out."

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.743
I'm just in, and then...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.578
I think it's --
-The Federman-and-out.

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:29.248
-I think it's 'cause my acting
is so precise and effervescent

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.550
that it just would ruin
the narrative arc

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:33.452
of any movie, so...
-[ Laughs ] That's interesting.

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:35.421
I want to talk about your book,

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:36.756
"The History of Stand-Up."
-Yes.

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:38.758
-I'm really excited
about this, buddy.

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.159
It is a fantastic book.

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:41.727
It's a great read.
It's funny.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:44.297
But I love that you did this!

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.099
I'm actually, like, kind of
jealous that you did this,

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:48.434
that I don't have the brain
to do this,

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:49.468
but 'cause...
-Aww.

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:51.870
-...I love every story in here.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.205
-Thank you so --
Thank you so much.

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:56.041
I -- It was a great thrill
to put it together.

00:02:56.041 --> 00:02:58.143
I've -- Again,
I've only done stand-up,

00:02:58.143 --> 00:03:00.212
so this is my first time
on this show as an author.

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:02.915
So I'll just start right here.
Chapter 1.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:04.584
"Who was the first comedian?"
-No, no, no.

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:06.486
Wayne, Wayne, Wayne, Wayne.
Wayne, Wayne, Wayne.

00:03:06.486 --> 00:03:08.521
That's not how this -- You don't
read the book on the show.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.922
That's not how we do it.
-Oh, I see.

00:03:09.922 --> 00:03:12.625
No, no, I just talk to you --
We talk about the book.

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:13.726
-You want me to go
to the ending?

00:03:13.726 --> 00:03:14.760
Do you want me
to tell you how it ends?

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.428
-No, I -- No.
No, thank you.

00:03:16.428 --> 00:03:17.964
I appreciate it.
No spoilers.

00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:20.566
It's the evolution
of stand-up comedy.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.468
-I know. And there
was people doing stand-up

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:24.570
before the term "stand-up"
even existed.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.839
So it was like --
it's really interesting.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.208
Like, Mark Twain, and there's
a guy called Artemus Ward.

00:03:29.208 --> 00:03:30.776
And I go through every era --
Vaudeville, nightclubs.

00:03:30.776 --> 00:03:33.412
-Now, Artemus -- I never
even knew anything about that.

00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:34.647
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:37.583
So, the term "stand-up"
came about in 1947,

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:40.286
but, like,
Bob Hope was doing stand-up,

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.655
you know, in the '30s
and in the '20s and stuff.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.624
So it's really interesting.

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:46.692
So we cover
all of the whole thing,

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:49.195
right up through,
you know, the comedy boom,

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:51.797
when you joined in, in the '90s.

00:03:51.797 --> 00:03:53.166
When did --
When did you start, Jimmy?

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:54.400
-Yeah, it probably was '90s.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.235
Yeah '90-- probably --
-In the '90s, yeah.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:58.104
So you were at, probably,
the comedy club.

00:03:58.104 --> 00:03:59.672
And so,
we cover right the whole --

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.640
right through the pandemic.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:05.111
We do the greats, you know,
Pryor and Moms Mabley.

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:08.214
And then we do some, like,
Wally Boag and Elsie Janis,

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.616
some people
you might not have known about.

00:04:10.616 --> 00:04:13.185
And, you know, I'm always --
a lot of it --

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:15.154
there's a lot of George Carlin
in this.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:18.490
It's just fun to think about,
like, Carlin as a young comic,

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.192
and then he became this legend,

00:04:20.192 --> 00:04:23.396
and, like, this story
keeps going over and over again.

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.564
And now there's new,
incredible comedians.

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:27.133
So, that's what the story is.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.469
-Wayne, before I give you the --
our outro here,

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:30.836
I just want to tell you that,
actually,

00:04:30.836 --> 00:04:32.104
you're one
of my favorite comedians

00:04:32.104 --> 00:04:34.340
that I've ever seen, ever.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.741
And I'm just
so honored and lucky

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:37.776
that I got to hang out with you
and -- and work with you.

00:04:37.776 --> 00:04:39.812
I really am a giant fan
of yours, dude.

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.948
-Ah, thank you.
Ah. That's incredible.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:43.282
I loved the way the audience

00:04:43.282 --> 00:04:45.417
burst into spontaneous applause
when you said that.

00:04:45.417 --> 00:04:47.119
[ Cheers and applause ]
That was -- That felt good.

00:04:47.119 --> 00:04:48.354
-Yeah, I don't know
what they were waiting for.

00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:51.324
-I --
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:51.324 --> 00:04:53.626
-Yeah, no, no,
this has been a blast.

00:04:53.626 --> 00:04:57.664
And the one thing I really wish
I was in studio for, Jimmy,

00:04:57.664 --> 00:05:00.733
is just the very, you know,
the fact that when you throw,

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.834
like, the host throws
to the commercial...

00:05:02.834 --> 00:05:04.336
-Yeah.
-...and then leans over

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:07.907
to the guest and does, like,
a little intimate conversation,

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:08.975
and the audience doesn't know --

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.209
no one knows
what they're saying.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:12.345
I wish we could have done that,
but we can't.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.447
But, anyway, thanks again
for having me on the show.

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:15.547
I really appreciate it.

00:05:15.547 --> 00:05:19.852
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:21.653
-I-I -- We can -- We can --

00:05:21.653 --> 00:05:23.222
I think we can try
to make something work out.

00:05:23.222 --> 00:05:24.223
Wayne Federman, everybody.

